WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.240
A neuron hears its survival signal at the far end of its axon.

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:09.320
How does the message get home?

3
00:00:10.283 --> 00:00:16.403
A sympathetic neuron collects NGF at axon terminals that can sit centimetres from

4
00:00:16.403 --> 00:00:17.403
its cell body.

5
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.667
The terminal hears the factor.

6
00:00:20.307 --> 00:00:22.667
But the nucleus decides whether the cell lives.

7
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:27.607
In these young neurons, dying needs new genes switched on in the nucleus.

8
00:00:28.587 --> 00:00:32.587
So a survival signal from the tip has to be able to act on the cell body.

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.480
Here is the idea to carry through.

10
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.260
A target keeps a neuron alive only if its signal makes the trip home.

11
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:43.593
And it must be able to signal when it gets there.

12
00:00:44.353 --> 00:00:47.813
On when it arrives, not necessarily on every second of the trip.

13
00:00:48.893 --> 00:00:50.133
Binding at the tip is not enough.

14
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.917
So could the signal simply drift home, by diffusion?

15
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.790
Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance.

16
00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:04.063
For a typical soluble protein and an axon one centimetre long, how long would the

17
00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:05.063
trip take?

18
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:12.287
Roughly two months.

19
00:01:13.107 --> 00:01:14.907
Over a few centimetres, many months.

20
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:21.287
Worse, many signals are proteins carrying a phosphate.

21
00:01:22.327 --> 00:01:24.827
Enzymes called phosphatases keep removing it.

22
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.170
Suppose a phosphate lasts about ten seconds.

23
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:34.030
Then the active signal falls to about a third every ten micrometres from the tip.

24
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.560
A centimetre is a thousand of those steps.

25
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.940
The rates are illustrative, but the conclusion holds.

26
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:43.383
It does not arrive late.

27
00:01:44.263 --> 00:01:45.303
It arrives switched off.

28
00:01:49.116 --> 00:01:51.476
So the signal needs a vehicle or a relay.

29
00:01:52.256 --> 00:01:54.356
And it must be able to signal when it arrives.

30
00:01:55.416 --> 00:01:57.356
One candidate fits both needs.

31
00:01:58.156 --> 00:02:01.036
At the tip, NGF binds its receptor, TrkA.

32
00:02:02.016 --> 00:02:06.896
Ligand and active receptor are pulled into the cell together, in a vesicle called

33
00:02:06.896 --> 00:02:08.236
the signaling endosome.

34
00:02:09.016 --> 00:02:10.576
It can keep signaling while carried.

35
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:16.150
The motor protein dynein drags it along microtubules toward the cell body.

36
00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:21.463
At roughly a micrometre each second, a centimetre takes a few hours.

37
00:02:22.516 --> 00:02:26.536
So what arrives must still be able to signal, after hours on the way.

38
00:02:27.456 --> 00:02:28.776
Carrying alone is not enough.

39
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.110
This model is favoured.

40
00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.470
But is it proven? Hold that question.

41
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.630
Takeaway: a survival signal must make it home still able to signal.
